=== CO5 TASK A5: HOW EASY IS IT TO TAKE THE SITE DOWN? ===
A5.1 — SYN Flood & DNS Amplification:
  - Backlog = 1024 half-open connections | Hold time = 60 s
  - Attack rate to exhaust server = 1024 / 60 = 17.07 SYN packets/sec (~18 SYN/s!)
  - How SYN cookies fix it: Server computes cryptographic ISN = hash(5-tuple, secret, MSS) and drops half-open
    state; TCB memory is allocated ONLY when client returns a valid ACK (so queue never fills).
  - Why DNS amplification is hard to block at victim: Attackers spoof victim's IP to open resolvers ->
    50-byte queries reflect 3,000+ byte ANY/TXT replies (50-70x amplification) that saturate the victim's
    access pipe BEFORE packets even reach the victim's firewall.

A5.2 — Layered Protection Plan:
  - SYN Flood         -> Enable TCP SYN Cookies on OS/load balancer + SYN proxy.
  - DNS Amplification -> Upstream Anycast Scrubbing Service / ISP BGP FlowSpec (drops junk before access link).
  - L7 HTTP Flood     -> CDN (offloads static/recorded video) + WAF / IP rate limiting.

--- Alternative Concept Analysis: Buying 4x Server Capacity ---
  - 4x servers absorb only 4 x 17.07 = 68.27 SYN packets/sec (~69 SYN/s).
  - Verdict: NOT realistic — a single laptop or small botnet easily exceeds 100,000+ SYN/s and saturates the WAN link.
